year="1981",
publisher="Springer Berlin Heidelberg",
address="Berlin, Heidelberg",
pages="167--183",
abstract="The paper is concerned with ways in which fair concurrency can be modelled using notations for omega-regular languages --- languages containing infinite sequences, whose recognizers are modified forms of B{\"u}chi or Muller-McNaughton automata. There are characterization of these languages in terms of recursion equation sets which involve both minimal and maximal fixpoint operators. The class of $\omega$-regular languages is closed under a fair concurrency operator. A general method for proving/deciding equivalences between such languages is obtained, derived from Milner's notion of ``simulation''.",
